(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-anaphylaxis-quiz"]={"title":"Anaphylaxis Treatment Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient in anaphylaxis is still wheezing after epinephrine. Which added drug relaxes bronchial smooth muscle?","choices":["Albuterol","Famotidine","Hydroxyzine","Prednisone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Albuterol, a beta-2 agonist bronchodilator, relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Besides constricting vessels and relaxing bronchial muscle, which signs of anaphylaxis does epinephrine alleviate?","choices":["Tremor, anxiousness and tachycardia","Hypertension and diaphoresis","Pruritus, urticaria and angioedema","Excess gastric acid secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond its alpha and beta effects, epinephrine alleviates pruritus, urticaria and angioedema. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which drug is given in anaphylaxis to block the systemic vasodilation and gastrointestinal effects of histamine?","choices":["Diphenhydramine","Hydroxyzine","Albuterol","Famotidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Famotidine, the histamine H2 blocker, blocks the systemic (vasodilation) and gastrointestinal (increased gastric acid secretion) effects of histamine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Why are corticosteroids not relied on for the immediate reversal of anaphylaxis?","choices":["They act on the skin signs alone","They cannot be given by injection","They worsen the hypotension","They take several hours to work"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids work on all aspects of anaphylaxis but take several hours to work. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which finding in anaphylaxis is the indication for intravenous crystalloid?","choices":["Widespread hives with itching","Profound vasodilation with hypotension","Wheezing from bronchospasm","Increased gastric acid secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous crystalloid (normal saline) is given for profound vasodilation and hypotension, because it increases intravascular volume. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which anaphylaxis drug lists hypertension and diaphoresis among its adverse effects?","choices":["Albuterol","Diphenhydramine","Epinephrine","Methylprednisolone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine's adverse effects are anxiety, hypertension, tachycardia, nausea and vomiting, and diaphoresis. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis by acting on which receptors?","choices":["Alpha and beta receptors","Beta-2 receptors","Histamine H1 receptors","Histamine H2 receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine acts on alpha receptors (vasoconstriction, decreased vascular permeability) and beta receptors (bronchial smooth muscle relaxation). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How can a severe case of anaphylaxis end?","choices":["In airway obstruction and circulatory collapse","In a slow, lasting decline in lung function","In an empyema or a lung abscess","In a thickening of the bronchial mucosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe anaphylaxis may result in complete obstruction of the airway, cardiovascular collapse and death. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"In anaphylaxis, which effects do histamine H1 blockers relieve?","choices":["Vasodilation and gastric acid","Rash, pruritus and hives","Bronchospasm and wheeze","Hypotension and shock"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The histamine H1 blockers diphenhydramine and hydroxyzine relieve the cutaneous effects: rash, pruritus and hives. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 53"}]};
